---
/**
 * The Outbuild AI Learning System as a standalone case study: one page to
 * link from a job application, in the homepage's dusk theme.
 *
 * Title, summary, problem, role, dates, stack, and the onboarding metric come
 * from the career dataset; the diagrams from portfolio-config. The prose is
 * condensed from the PDF write-up (public/assets/projects/), which stays the
 * long version.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseFigure from '../../components/portfolio/CaseFigure.astro';
import Lightbox from '../../components/portfolio/Lightbox.astro';
import ContactFooter from '../../components/portfolio/ContactFooter.astro';
import { portfolioConfig } from '../../data/portfolio/portfolio-config';
import { getAchievement, getExperience, getProject, getRoles, monthRange } from '../../lib/career';

const p = getProject('proj_outbuild_learning');
const exp = getExperience(p.related_experience_id!);
const role = getRoles(exp)[0];
const metric = (name: string) => getAchievement('outbuild_learning')?.metrics?.find((m) => m.name === name);
const before = metric('onboarding_before');
const after = metric('onboarding_after');
const pdf = p.links?.find((l) => l.kind === 'pdf');
const slides = portfolioConfig.create.figures[p.id] ?? [];
const fig = 'outbuild';
---

<Base
  title={`${p.title} · Case study`}
  description={p.website_summary ?? p.summary}
  slimNav={false}
  ogImage="/assets/projects/outbuild/og.jpg"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="preload" as="image" href="/assets/forest/dusk/master.webp" />
    <link href="/assets/css/dusk.css" rel="stylesheet" />
    <link href="/assets/css/slideshow.css" rel="stylesheet" />
    <link href="/assets/css/case.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  </Fragment>

  <article class="case dusk-content">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <nav class="case-nav" aria-label="Site">
        <a class="case-brand" href="/">Kaung Thiha</a>
        <a class="case-back" href="/#create"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> More of my work</a>
      </nav>
      <div class="case-col case-hero-text">
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Case study · {p.category}</p>
        <h1 class="case-title">{p.title}</h1>
        <p class="case-lede">{p.website_summary}</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-col">
      <dl class="case-facts">
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>{role.canonical_title}{exp.engagement_type && <span> ({exp.engagement_type})</span>}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Company</dt><dd>{exp.organization}{exp.organization_description && <span class="case-fact-sub">{exp.organization_description}</span>}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Timeline</dt><dd>{monthRange(role)}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Stack</dt><dd>{p.technologies?.join(' · ')}</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <ul class="case-stats">
        {before && after && (
          <li><span class="case-stat">{before.value} <span class="case-stat-arrow" aria-label="to">→</span> {after.value}</span> weeks to onboard a new AE or SDR</li>
        )}
        <li><span class="case-stat">6</span> knowledge domains, 10–15 questions each</li>
        <li><span class="case-stat">0</span> quizzes written or graded by hand after setup</li>
      </ul>

      <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="problem">
        <h2 id="problem">The problem</h2>
        <p>{p.problem}</p>
        <p>
          I joined Outbuild to clean up HubSpot workflows and data hygiene, but onboarding looked like a problem
          worth solving with AI. The founding team had already built a thorough Notion knowledge base: construction
          fundamentals, scheduling methods, the competitive landscape, positioning, and sales playbooks.
        </p>
        <p>
          But onboarding meant reading the wiki, sitting in on calls, and asking questions. There was no structured
          assessment, no way to spot knowledge gaps, no feedback for the rep, and no visibility for leadership, who
          didn't have time to coach every hire beyond 1:1s. New hires were "ramped" when they felt ready, which
          varied wildly from person to person.
        </p>
        <h3>What it had to do</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Scale to any number of reps without anyone writing or grading quizzes by hand.</li>
          <li>Cover six domains: construction basics, scheduling and planning, project management solutions, other construction tech, Outbuild positioning and messaging, and the AE playbook.</li>
          <li>Give reps immediate, specific feedback, so they know exactly what to review.</li>
          <li>Show leadership team-wide progress and knowledge gaps.</li>
          <li>Run on tools the team already used (Notion and Google Workspace), and keep working without an engineer as the knowledge base grows.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <CaseFigure slides={slides} title="Problem" lightbox={fig} />

    <div class="case-col">
      <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="approach">
        <h2 id="approach">The approach</h2>
        <p>
          My first instinct was a quiz tool like Typeform with hand-built quizzes. That fell apart quickly: writing
          10–15 questions per topic across six domains is tedious, the questions go stale as the knowledge base
          changes, and grading and feedback would still be manual.
        </p>
        <p>
          The bigger win was automating the whole loop, from reading the knowledge base to delivering results, with
          Claude as the intelligence layer and n8n orchestrating the flow. (I'd used Zapier before, but the team
          already knew n8n.) The final system is three pipelines that run independently:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Quiz generation.</strong> Every day, it finds knowledge-base pages without a quiz, pulls their content through the Notion API, and has Claude write 10–15 multiple-choice questions as structured JSON: options, correct answers, explanations, and difficulty. A code step turns that into a Google Forms API batch request to build the form, and the answer key is saved to Notion.</li>
          <li><strong>Grading.</strong> When a rep submits, an Apps Script trigger calls an n8n webhook. The pipeline looks up the answer key, has Claude grade the responses, writes the score, pass/fail, weak areas, and recommendations to a Notion results database, and emails the rep their results.</li>
          <li><strong>Reporting.</strong> Every Monday, Claude turns the week's results into an HTML email for leadership: completion and pass rates, average scores, individual progress, knowledge gaps, and recommendations for the onboarding team.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <CaseFigure slides={slides} title="Final system" lightbox={fig} />

    <div class="case-col">
      <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="under-the-hood">
        <h2 id="under-the-hood">Under the hood</h2>
        <p>Claude does three jobs, each with its own system prompt:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Writing quizzes.</strong> The prompt asks for 10–15 questions with four options each, a mix of recall, applied, and scenario-based questions, and 2–3 questions per topic on where Outbuild fits in. Claude returns pure JSON, since it can't call the Forms API itself.</li>
          <li><strong>Grading.</strong> One point per correct choice on multi-select questions and −0.5 per incorrect one, plus weak areas and learning recommendations.</li>
          <li><strong>Reporting.</strong> An HTML email built from the raw results.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Three Notion databases tie it together: the knowledge base (which pages have quizzes), answer keys (the
          rubric for each form), and quiz results. Google Forms won over other quiz tools because it plugs into
          Google Sheets, which the team already used, and needs no new accounts for a team on Google Workspace.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <CaseFigure slides={slides} title="Implementation" lightbox={fig} />

    <div class="case-col">
      <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="v1-v2">
        <h2 id="v1-v2">V1 → V2</h2>
        <p>
          The first version proved the idea but didn't scale. It ran six parallel branches, one per topic, each with
          its own trigger, filter, sync logic, and Notion update: about 36 nodes doing the same thing. Quiz content
          was hardcoded into the Claude prompt, score denominators were hardcoded per topic (66, 46, 21…) so any
          question change broke grading, and a manual "Synced At" column in Sheets tracked what had been processed.
        </p>
        <p>
          V2 collapses all of that into one topic-agnostic pipeline of about 20 nodes. Content comes live from the
          Notion API, so a new knowledge-base page gets a quiz automatically. Answer keys live in Notion as JSON,
          webhooks replace the sync column, and grading moved from raw Forms scores to Claude, with partial credit
          and explanations.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <CaseFigure slides={slides} title="V1 → V2" lightbox={fig} />

    <div class="case-col">
      <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="outcomes">
        <h2 id="outcomes">Outcomes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Onboarding went from about four weeks of unstructured learning to about one week of guided, assessment-driven learning.</li>
          <li>Six domains covered at 10–15 questions each, with no manual quiz writing, grading, or reporting after setup. The system was designed for 20+ concurrent users.</li>
          <li>Reps who failed a quiz knew exactly which Notion pages to revisit, retook it, and could show concrete progress.</li>
          <li>Leadership could see which topics were hardest for new hires (consistently: lean construction terminology and competitive differentiation).</li>
          <li>The knowledge base got better too. If Claude couldn't write good questions from a page, the page needed better content.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <CaseFigure slides={slides} title="Outcomes" lightbox={fig} />

    <div class="case-col">
      <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="learned">
        <h2 id="learned">What I learned</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Output quality lives in the prompt.</strong> The gap between "generate quiz questions" and the final prompt (difficulty tiers, Outbuild-specific questions, scenario formats) decided whether the quizzes were useful.</li>
          <li><strong>Let n8n connect APIs and let Claude do the thinking.</strong> V1 had chunky code nodes for text extraction; V2 keeps nodes clean.</li>
          <li><strong>Webhooks beat sync state.</strong> The "Synced At" column meant a full read-and-check cycle for every response.</li>
          <li><strong>Hardcoded denominators stink.</strong> Storing the answer key and total points with the form ID let grading handle quiz updates gracefully.</li>
          <li><strong>Start from the executive report and work backwards.</strong> Defining what leadership wanted to see (pass rates by topic, individual progress, knowledge gaps) clarified what the system needed to capture.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <div class="case-end">
        {pdf && (
          <p class="case-more">
            The full write-up has the n8n workflows and the Notion schema.
            <a class="text-link" href={pdf.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read it (PDF) →</a>
          </p>
        )}
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/#create"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> More of my work</a></p>
        <ContactFooter />
      </div>
    </div>

    <Lightbox slides={slides} label={`${p.title} diagrams`} id={fig} />
  </article>
</Base>
